# Local agent elevation: sudo with Navi phone approval

Machine policy: for agentic development on this Gentoo/KDE workstation, local root
commands run through the installed `/usr/bin/sudo`. The user approves each command
on the enrolled phone (Navi) with a fingerprint. This applies across projects and
agents. It is a choice of authentication route, not blanket authorization for
privileged actions.

## Choose the route

- Run ordinary builds, source edits and readable diagnostics as the desktop user.
- For an authorized command requiring local root, use exactly this form:

  ```sh
  SUDO_ASKPASS=/bin/false script -q -E never -ec "sudo -A <exact root command>" /dev/null < /dev/null
  ```

  - `script` gives sudo a terminal. Navi's sudo approval only works in a terminal
    under the user's Konsole or Kitty, and agent shells have none of their own.
  - `-E never` stops `script` from echoing a stray control character into the output.
  - `sudo -A` with `SUDO_ASKPASS=/bin/false` makes the password fallback fail at once.
    The phone check runs before any password is asked. Without this, a rejection or
    timeout leaves sudo waiting at `Password:` until its own timeout.
  - Run it with a tool timeout of at least 180 seconds (see "Observe the result").
- Keep the existing tool sandbox and approval checks. A tool's `require_escalated`
  setting requests sandbox access; it does not make the process root. When needed,
  obtain that tool approval and use the sudo form inside it.
- Do not use kdesu, pkexec, su, doas, plain `sudo` without `-A`, `sudo -n`, `sudo -S`,
  or a direct authority-helper call as an automatic alternative. The only other form
  is the password-only one, and only when the phone is unreachable (see "When an
  approval does not arrive"). If the sudo route fails otherwise, diagnose it and
  report the concrete blocker. Another route requires the user's explicit choice.
- Do not try to make approvals last longer. Each wrapped command runs on a new
  terminal, so every command needs its own phone approval; keep it that way. Do not
  change sudoers (`timestamp_type`, `timestamp_timeout`, NOPASSWD) or PAM to avoid it.
- An approval already granted for the concrete operation in the conversation remains
  valid. Do not ask for the same conversational approval again just because sudo is
  used. The phone approval is still required for the actual launch.
- This policy concerns local host elevation. Remote hosts and containers keep their
  own explicitly authorized authentication mechanisms.

## Write the command

- Quote the command as one argument inside the `script -c` string, using a proper
  quoting function (Python `shlex.join`/`shlex.quote`) when composing it. Put
  redirections inside the root command (`sudo -A sh -c '… > /root/file'`) so they
  happen as root. Avoid double expansion by the outer shell.
- The phone shows the full command; review is the point. Commands up to the installed
  limit (default 10240 characters, `MAX_COMMAND` in
  `/usr/libexec/navi-auth/host.py`) are offered. A longer command is not offered to
  the phone: sudo falls back to the password, which fails immediately here.
- Multi-line scripts and tabs are allowed. Navi numbers real lines and marks display
  breaks after `&&`, `||`, `;` and `|`. Prefer several short, readable commands to one
  long one. Do not hide a payload in an encoding (base64, compressed or escaped
  blobs): the user must be able to read what runs.
- A script file run by path (`sudo -A sh /path/script`) shows only the path, not its
  contents. Pass the contents instead (`sudo -A sh -c "$(cat file)"`), or keep the
  script in a root-owned location and verify its digest within the same root command.

## Observe the result

- sudo returns the command's real exit status, and its output comes back through the
  terminal. Lines end in `\r\n` (from the terminal); strip `\r` when parsing.
- Every request prints its own short ID first, for example
  `Approve this sudo in Navi on your phone… (request 1e4d61b1; …)`. Several agents can
  ask at the same time: each one reads only the lines for its own ID. Navi sends one
  request to the phone at a time; a later one prints `Waiting for another Navi request
  to finish… (request …)` and is sent when the earlier one is decided (it gives up
  after 60 seconds of waiting).
- Allow up to 60 seconds of waiting plus 60 seconds for the approval: use a tool
  timeout of at least 180 seconds.
- The terminal states the outcome in one line, and the journal records it under the
  same ID (`journalctl -t navi-auth --since -10min -o cat | grep <ID>`; the journal
  lines carry the full ID as `desktop=<ID>…`):

| Terminal line | Journal (`sudo-ended … outcome=`) | Meaning | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Approved on the phone — request <ID>.` | `sudo-approved` | Approved; the command ran | Use the command's own exit status and output. |
| `Not approved on the phone (rejected)` | `outcome=rejected` | **Rejected** on the phone | Do not retry. Do not resend it unchanged; ask, or adjust what was asked for. |
| `Not approved on the phone (timed out)` | `outcome=timed-out` | Reached the phone but not answered before it expired | Retry (see below). |
| `Not approved on the phone (phone unreachable)` | `outcome=unreachable` (and `phone-unavailable …`) | No route to the phone | Use the password-only form (see below). |
| `Not approved on the phone (busy: …)` | `sudo-skipped: another Navi request is in progress …` | Another request kept the phone busy for 60 s | Wait for it, then send again; this does not count as a retry. |
| none of these; sudo fails | `sudo-skipped: …` | Not offered to the phone (for example over the command limit) | Fix the cause; do not resend unchanged. |

In every "Not approved" case the command did not run.

## When an approval does not arrive

### Timed out: retry, at most three times

- Retry the identical command up to **three times**, one request at a time, each in a
  fresh run of the sudo form. Before each retry, tell the user briefly that the
  approval timed out and that the request is being sent again.
- **If the third retry also times out** (four requests in total), stop. Do not send
  another request on your own. Tell the user the approval timed out four times and
  ask them to say when they are ready to watch for it; resend only after they reply.
  Their reply starts a new count of three retries.
- A rejection at any point ends the retries. Never retry automatically after a
  rejection or a skipped request.

### Phone unreachable: password only

When the outcome is `phone unreachable`, run the same command once in the
password-only form. It shows the KDE password dialog on the desktop for the user to
type the administrator password, with no phone involved:

```sh
SUDO_ASKPASS=/usr/bin/ksshaskpass sudo -A <exact root command> < /dev/null
```

- No `script` wrapper: without a terminal Navi does not offer the phone, so sudo goes
  straight to the password, asked by `ksshaskpass`. The output and the real exit
  status come back as usual.
- Tell the user first that the phone is unreachable and a password dialog is coming.
- If the user cancels the dialog or the password fails, stop and report it. Do not
  retry and do not switch to another route.
- Use this form only after an `unreachable` outcome (or when the user asks for it),
  never as a first choice and never after a rejection or a timeout.

## Preserve the installed phone path

- The phone and desktop enrollment already work. Do not rebuild or re-enroll the
  phone, rotate keys, change PAM, sudoers or Navi settings, or install another root
  service to solve an ordinary elevation request.
- Use the real desktop session environment; do not manufacture another user's
  session environment.

## Package operations and handoffs

Use the Gentoo workflow: inspect and accept the exact Portage plan before merging.
For an already accepted plan, run emerge noninteractively through the sudo form
(`emerge … --ask=n`), with output capture. Do not run `emerge --ask` behind it.

When writing an installer or administrative script, make its local elevation path
this sudo form, or require the caller to launch it that way. Inspect existing
scripts for internal pkexec/kdesu/sudo calls before running them. Preserve their
review and content-verification checks when adapting them.

Carry this policy into agent handoffs, generated command examples and new local
agent configurations. After a session restart or compaction, reload the policy
before the next privileged action.
